\begin{question}
\textbf{The normal distribution in R}

A machine fills coffee into cups. The filled amount $X$ (in ml) is normally distributed with $\mu=200$ and $\sigma=5$.

\begin{parts}
\item Use \texttt{pnorm()} to compute $P(X<190)$ and $P(195\leq X\leq 205)$.
\item Use \texttt{qnorm()} to find the amount $x$ that is undercut by only 5\% of all cups.
\item Standardize: compute $P(X<190)$ a second time using \texttt{pnorm()} with its default arguments \texttt{mean = 0} and \texttt{sd = 1}.
\item Simulate $10{,}000$ fillings with \texttt{rnorm()}. Estimate $P(X<190)$ and the 5\% quantile from the simulated values (\emph{hint:} \texttt{mean()} of a logical vector and \texttt{quantile()}), and compare with (a) and (b).
\item Plot the densities of $\mathcal{N}(200, 5^2)$, $\mathcal{N}(210, 5^2)$ and $\mathcal{N}(200, 10^2)$ in one plot using \texttt{curve()} and \texttt{dnorm()}. How do $\mu$ and $\sigma$ change the shape?
\end{parts}
\end{question}

\begin{solution}
\begin{parts}
\item
<<echo=TRUE>>=
pnorm(190, mean = 200, sd = 5)
pnorm(205, mean = 200, sd = 5) - pnorm(195, mean = 200, sd = 5)
@
\item
<<echo=TRUE>>=
qnorm(0.05, mean = 200, sd = 5)
@
\item
<<echo=TRUE>>=
pnorm((190 - 200) / 5)
@
\item
<<echo=TRUE>>=
x_sim <- rnorm(10000, mean = 200, sd = 5)
mean(x_sim < 190)          # relative frequency of cups below 190 ml
quantile(x_sim, 0.05)      # empirical 5% quantile
@
Both are close to the exact values from (a) and (b).
\item
\setkeys{Gin}{width=0.55\textwidth}
<<normplot, echo=TRUE, fig=TRUE, height=3.5, width=6, eps=FALSE>>=
curve(dnorm(x, mean = 200, sd = 5),  from = 170, to = 240,
      lwd = 2, xlab = "x", ylab = "Density")
curve(dnorm(x, mean = 210, sd = 5),  add = TRUE, lwd = 2, col = "red")
curve(dnorm(x, mean = 200, sd = 10), add = TRUE, lwd = 2, col = "blue")
legend("topright", legend = c("N(200, 25)", "N(210, 25)", "N(200, 100)"),
       col = c("black", "red", "blue"), lwd = 2, bty = "n")
@

Changing $\mu$ shifts the bell curve (location); a larger $\sigma$ makes it wider and flatter (spread).
\end{parts}
\end{solution}

